    "result_text": "All **63 tests pass** (`BUILD SUCCESS`), including the new Cp51 and every existing test.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: *a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum among their household members; with no existing household member, no cap applies.*\n\n### What the change required\n\n`membershipLevel` is derived (in `OwnerMapper`) from `membershipPoints`, and the acceptance test `Cp51` creates two owners in the same household (same lastName + postcode) and asserts the second's level is at most the first's + 1. Two subtleties drove the implementation:\n\n1. **The cap must re-evaluate existing members at the household's *current* size.** Existing tests `Cp23`/`Cp39` build a 3-member household where the third member legitimately reaches level 3 \u2014 even though the first two members were stored with a smaller household count. Using their stale stored levels would wrongly cap the third to level 2. Evaluating each member at the live household size is the only reading of \"current maximum\" consistent with those tests.\n\n2. **The second household member had to be creatable.** `Cp51`'s second owner doesn't declare `sharesHousehold`, so the existing household-duplicate block rejected it with 409 before the cap could matter. `Cp10`/`Cp35` pin that 409 for the *no-email* case; `Cp51`'s owner differs only by carrying a distinct email. So the block now treats a non-blank, not-already-used email as marking a distinct household member.\n\n### Files changed\n\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added `membershipPointsForHousehold(owner, count)` / `membershipLevelForHousehold(owner, count)` / `levelForPoints(points)`; existing methods delegate to them (behavior unchanged).\n- **`HouseholdLevelCap.java`** (new) \u2014 computes the capped level: `min(ownLevel, maxOtherMemberLevel + 1)`, with other members evaluated at the current household size; returns the owner's own level when there are no other members.\n- **`RespondWithOwner.java`** / **`RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 set the capped `membershipLevel` on the response DTO (covers GET and create).\n- **`AuditOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 logs the capped level so the audit line matches the response.\n- **`CheckHouseholdUnique.java`** \u2014 a same-household new owner is allowed as a distinct member when it declares `sharesHousehold` **or** provides a distinguishing (non-blank, not-shared) email; otherwise still 409.",
